# 1️⃣3️⃣ BST Predecessor

The **inorder predecessor** of a node is the largest value smaller than that node.

Example:

```text
Inorder:

3 5 7 8 10 12 15
```

Predecessor of:

```text
10
```

is:

```text
8
```

---

# 1️⃣4️⃣ BST Successor

The **inorder successor** of a node is the smallest value greater than that node.

Example:

```text
3 5 7 8 10 12 15
```

Successor of:

```text
10
```

is:

```text
12
```

---

# 1️⃣5️⃣ BST Search Pattern

Whenever searching for a value:

```text
target < root
→ LEFT

target > root
→ RIGHT

target == root
→ FOUND
```

Remember:

```text
        root
       /    \
   smaller  greater
```

This is the core idea behind most BST operations.

---

# 1️⃣6️⃣ BST Insert Pattern

For insertion:

```text
value < root
→ Insert Left

value > root
→ Insert Right

root == NULL
→ Create Node
```

---

# 1️⃣7️⃣ Minimum & Maximum Pattern

Remember:

```text
Minimum
→ Keep moving LEFT

Maximum
→ Keep moving RIGHT
```

Visual:

```text
          8
        /   \
       5     12
      / \    / \
     3   7  10  15
    ↑              ↑
   MIN            MAX
```

---

# 1️⃣8️⃣ BST Height and Complexity

The complexity of BST operations depends on the height of the tree.

If the BST is balanced:

```text
h ≈ log n
```

Therefore:

```text
Search    → O(log n)

Insert    → O(log n)

Delete    → O(log n)
```

In the worst case, the BST can become skewed:

```text
1
 \
  2
   \
    3
     \
      4
```

Then:

```text
h = n
```

So:

```text
Search → O(n)

Insert → O(n)

Delete → O(n)
```

---

# 📊 BST Complexity Cheat Sheet

| Operation | Average | Worst Case |
|-----------|---------|------------|
| Search | O(log n) | O(n) |
| Insert | O(log n) | O(n) |
| Find Min | O(log n) | O(n) |
| Find Max | O(log n) | O(n) |
| Inorder Traversal | O(n) | O(n) |

